<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study universal online convex optimization with delayed feedback. We develop a single algorithm that simultaneously adapts to general convex, strongly convex, and exp-concave losses without prior knowledge of the curvature class or parameters. Our regret bounds match the best known dependence on the time horizon <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and delays up to doubly logarithmic factors: roughly <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for convex losses and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}\log T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}\log T</annotation></semantics></math> rates with curved losses, where <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{tot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{tot}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total cumulative delay and <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of simultaneously missing observations. Our main technical contribution is a delayed meta-learner with a second-order excess-loss guarantee, which enables curvature-adaptive aggregation of delayed base learners. In addition, our new exp-concave base learner yields a sharper regret bound which improves the state-of-the-art delay dependence by replacing the maximum delay with the potentially much smaller <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, while retaining the complementary <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Online convex optimization (OCO)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hazan, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orabona, 2026</a>)</cite> provides a standard framework for sequential decision making under uncertainty. On each round <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, the learner selects a decision <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from a convex decision set <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, incurs loss <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, and receives feedback before making their next decision. The objective is to minimize regret, which measures the cumulative loss difference relative to the best fixed decision in hindsight. The attainable regret rate depends on the curvature of the losses. General convex losses admit the minimax rate <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zinkevich, 2003</a>)</cite>, whereas strongly convex and exp-concave losses admit a faster <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hazan et al., 2007</a>)</cite>.
Classical algorithms exploit this curvature through updates and learning rates tailored to the loss class and its curvature parameter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, these loss properties may be unknown before learning begins. This motivates <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal OCO</em>, which seeks a single learner that automatically attains the appropriate regret guarantee without this prior knowledge. A sequence of works has developed such algorithms for general convex, strongly convex, and exp-concave losses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Erven, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These results show that a learner can benefit from curvature without knowing in advance whether it is present or how strong it is.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although these universal guarantees assume immediate feedback, many applications involve <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">delayed</em> feedback: the feedback generated in one round is revealed only several rounds later. Such delays arise naturally in distributed and asynchronous optimization, where computation and communication latency lead to stale gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and in online advertising, where conversions may be observed long after a user clicks an advertisement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Existing studies have developed algorithms for delayed OCO with general convex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi, 2015</a>)</cite>, strongly convex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and exp-concave losses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These results have shown that curvature can still improve regret rates under delayed feedback. However, the algorithms that exploit it still require prior knowledge of the loss class and its curvature parameter. This leaves the following question open:</p>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can a single algorithm adapt near-optimally to unknown curvature under delayed feedback?</span></p>
</div>
