<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we answer the above question in the affirmative. Specifically:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3" title="3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we first propose <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Delayed ML-Prod</em>, a meta-algorithm that provides a second-order excess-loss guarantee under delayed feedback. This guarantee allows curvature to control the cost of aggregation, enabling a single learner to inherit the regret guarantees of appropriately tuned base learners.
Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we keep the expert weights associated with unresolved rounds unchanged while adjusting their contributions according to the amount of missing feedback. Each delayed update is then applied to the weights used to form the corresponding prediction, preserving the connection between predictions and updates needed for the second-order guarantee.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Improved Regret Bound for Exp-concave Losses ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we next develop an improved base learner for exp-concave losses with a known curvature parameter <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, we propose <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Delay-Accounting ONS</em>, a delayed variant of ONS that achieves
<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}\left(n\log T+\min\left\{n\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}\left(n\log T+\min\left\{n\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
regret, where <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{tot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{tot}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total delay, and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of concurrently pending observations at the start of a round. This improves the bound of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a></cite> by replacing the maximum delay <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> factor in the logarithmic branch with the potentially much smaller <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, while retaining the complementary <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}</annotation></semantics></math> guarantee. The key idea is to extend the weighting of unresolved rounds to the quadratic ONS surrogates and use adaptive regularization to obtain the best of both delay-dependent bounds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Universal Regret Guarantee ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we use Delayed ML-Prod to combine delayed OGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi, 2015</a>)</cite>, delayed FTRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and our Delay-Accounting ONS subroutine.
The resulting single algorithm adapts to general convex, strongly convex, and exp-concave losses without prior knowledge of the loss class or its curvature parameter. To our knowledge, this is the first such algorithm for delayed OCO that simultaneously adapts to all three loss classes. Its regret bounds, summarized in <a href="#S1.T1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, match the corresponding class-specific guarantees up to doubly logarithmic factors in <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Related works</h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Online learning with delayed feedback.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Online convex optimization with delayed feedback has been studied through both generic reductions and direct algorithmic extensions. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joulani et al. (2013)</a></cite> proposed BOLD, which reduces online learning with delayed feedback to the non-delayed setting by running independent copies of a base learner. For general convex losses, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi (2015)</a></cite> proposed delayed online gradient descent and obtained <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})</annotation></semantics></math> regret. For strongly convex losses with a known curvature parameter, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2022)</a></cite> proposed DOGD-SC and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed SDMD-RSC, both achieving <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}((d_{\max}+1)\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}((d_{\max}+1)\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> regret. Most closely related to our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a></cite> developed a delayed FTRL algorithm that improves this guarantee to <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\log T+\min\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\log T+\min\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\})</annotation></semantics></math>. They also proposed a delayed ONS algorithm achieving <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(n\log T+\min\{d_{\max}n\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(n\log T+\min\{d_{\max}n\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\})</annotation></semantics></math> regret for exp-concave losses. We use their delayed FTRL algorithm as a base learner and improve the delay dependence of ONS before combining these guarantees through a universal learner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prediction with expert advice has also been studied under delayed feedback. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korotin et al. (2020)</a></cite> developed delayed variants of Hedge and Fixed Share and established corresponding regret guarantees. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoeven (2022)</a></cite> obtained a second-order regret bound in terms of the losses, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van der Hoeven (2022)</a></cite> established a first-order regret bound for experts under delayed feedback. In contrast, our Delayed ML-Prod builds on the non-delayed ML-Prod <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaillard and Stoltz, 2014</a>)</cite> and provides a second-order regret bound in terms of the excess losses, thereby enabling us to exploit curvature when aggregating the base learners.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond full-gradient feedback, delays have also been studied under bandit feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cesa-Bianchi et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zimmert and Seldin, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van der Hoeven et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Particularly relevant to our algorithm design is Banker-OMD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a framework for multi-armed bandits with delayed feedback. Its key idea is to construct new decisions by combining available mirror-descent updates from multiple past rounds in the dual space. We adopt a similar weighting principle in designing Delayed ML-Prod and our ONS subroutine, combining historical expert weights and quadratic surrogate functions, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Universal online convex optimization.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Universal OCO aims to obtain regret guarantees appropriate to the loss class without prior knowledge of the class or its curvature parameters. MetaGrad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Erven, 2016</a>)</cite> adapts to unknown curvature through multiple learning rates, while Maler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> achieves optimal guarantees for general convex, strongly convex, and exp-concave losses. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a></cite> developed a general framework that also inherits problem-dependent guarantees from existing algorithms. Subsequent work incorporated gradient variation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or reduced the projection cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These methods establish universality under immediate feedback, whereas our work studies curvature adaptation under delayed feedback.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem Setting</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be the time horizon and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the dimension.
We consider online convex optimization with delayed feedback over a nonempty convex
decision set <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At each round <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, the learner chooses <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and incurs
a loss <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for some convex loss function <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The gradient <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}\coloneq\nabla f_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}\coloneq\nabla f_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as well as its associated timestamp <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is revealed at the end of round <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-negative integer that is unknown to the learner.
Since feedback received at or after the end of round <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> cannot affect
any decision, we may truncate each delay at <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we assume <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+d_{t}\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+d_{t}\leq T</annotation></semantics></math> without loss of generality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joulani et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joulani et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
For any comparator <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the learner’s regret is defined as
<math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)\coloneq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\bigl(f_{t}(x_{t})-f_{t}(u)\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)\coloneq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\bigl(f_{t}(x_{t})-f_{t}(u)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of round <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}\coloneq\{\tau\in[t-1]:\tau+d_{\tau}&lt;t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}\coloneq\{\tau\in[t-1]:\tau+d_{\tau}&lt;t\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}\coloneq[t-1]\setminus o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}\coloneq[t-1]\setminus o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sets of past rounds with observed and missing feedback, respectively.
Moreover, define
<math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}\coloneq o_{t+1}\setminus o_{t}=\{\tau\in[t]:\tau+d_{\tau}=t\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}\coloneq o_{t+1}\setminus o_{t}=\{\tau\in[t]:\tau+d_{\tau}=t\},</annotation></semantics></math>
as the set of rounds whose feedback is revealed at the end of round <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the maximum delay, total delay, and maximum number of missing observations up to round <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}\coloneq\max_{t\in[T]}d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}\coloneq\max_{t\in[T]}d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{tot}}\coloneq\sum_{t=1}^{T}d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{tot}}\coloneq\sum_{t=1}^{T}d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\coloneq\max_{s\in[t]}|m_{s}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\coloneq\max_{s\in[t]}|m_{s}|</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and let <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}\coloneq\sigma_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}\coloneq\sigma_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}\coloneq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}\coloneq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For each round <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}\coloneq\{t,t+1,\ldots,t+d_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}\coloneq\{t,t+1,\ldots,t+d_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be its delay
interval and denote <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{t}\coloneq 1+\max_{s\in I_{t}}|m_{s}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{t}\coloneq 1+\max_{s\in I_{t}}|m_{s}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
By definition, we have <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{t}\leq\sigma_{\max}+1\coloneq\sigma_{\max}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{t}\leq\sigma_{\max}+1\coloneq\sigma_{\max}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the paper, we impose the following boundedness assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bounded domain and gradients)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, convex, and compact, contains the
origin, and has diameter
<math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\coloneq\max_{x,y\in\mathcal{X}}\|x-y\|_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\coloneq\max_{x,y\in\mathcal{X}}\|x-y\|_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, with
<math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla f_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq G,\forall x\in\mathcal{X},t\in[T]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla f_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq G,\forall x\in\mathcal{X},t\in[T].</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to general convex losses, we consider the following two curvature conditions for the loss functions. Note that the learner knows neither the curvature class of the loss sequence nor the corresponding curvature parameter in advance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmassumption2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-strong convexity)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For some <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies that <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x,y\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x,y\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(y)\geq f_{t}(x)+\langle\nabla f_{t}(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|y-x\|_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(y)\geq f_{t}(x)+\langle\nabla f_{t}(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|y-x\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmassumption3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-exp-concavity)<span id="Thmassumption3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For some <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-exp-concave on
<math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto\exp(-\alpha f_{t}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto\exp(-\alpha f_{t}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> is concave
over <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other notations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any vector <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v^{1},\ldots,v^{n})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v^{1},\ldots,v^{n})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a finite set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{H})\coloneq\{q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{|\mathcal{H}|}:\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}q^{h}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{H})\coloneq\{q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{|\mathcal{H}|}:\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}q^{h}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the probability simplex over <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{int}\Delta(\mathcal{H})\coloneq\{q\in\Delta(\mathcal{H}):q^{h}&gt;0,\ \forall h\in\mathcal{H}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>int</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{int}\Delta(\mathcal{H})\coloneq\{q\in\Delta(\mathcal{H}):q^{h}&gt;0,\ \forall h\in\mathcal{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote its relative interior.
We use <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the all-ones vector, with its dimension determined by the context, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix.
For a positive semidefinite matrix <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, define
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{A}\coloneq\sqrt{x^{\top}Ax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{A}\coloneq\sqrt{x^{\top}Ax}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite, define its dual norm by
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{A^{-1}}\coloneq\sqrt{x^{\top}A^{-1}x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>≔</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{A^{-1}}\coloneq\sqrt{x^{\top}A^{-1}x}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}\{\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}\{\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indicator function and, for each subset <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subseteq\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subseteq\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{H}\coloneq(\mathbb{I}\{h\in H\})_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\in\{0,1\}^{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{H}\coloneq(\mathbb{I}\{h\in H\})_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\in\{0,1\}^{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the indicator vector of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h}\coloneq\mathbf{1}_{\{h\}}\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h}\coloneq\mathbf{1}_{\{h\}}\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math> the standard-basis vector indexed by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by introducing the meta-algorithm for OCO with delayed feedback,
which aggregates the decisions of a finite collection of experts while
accounting for delayed observations.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{1,2,\dots,|\mathcal{H}|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{1,2,\dots,|\mathcal{H}|\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of experts.
We further assume to have a prior distribution <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>
over the experts, such that each expert <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned a positive prior
mass <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{h}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{h}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Protocol.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce the protocol of expert aggregation with delayed feedback.
On round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, each expert <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> proposes a feasible decision
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{h}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{h}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> based only on the feedback revealed before time <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the same feedback information, the meta-algorithm chooses a
distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\in\Delta(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math> and plays the feasible mixed action
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}q_{t}^{h}x_{t}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}q_{t}^{h}x_{t}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The gradient <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}=\nabla f_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}=\nabla f_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is then revealed only at the end of
round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> due to the presence of delays.
Once the gradient <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is revealed, define the meta-loss
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}^{h}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}^{h}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for every base expert <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}^{h}\coloneq(\left\langle g_{t},x_{t}^{h}-x_{t}\right\rangle+GD)/(2GD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}^{h}\coloneq(\left\langle g_{t},x_{t}^{h}-x_{t}\right\rangle+GD)/(2GD)</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we scale
the meta-losses to be in <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> in order to
match the classical prediction-with-expert-advice formulation.
Our algorithm and analysis are
invariant to such constant shifts and rescaling.</span></span></span>
Then, the incurred loss of the meta-algorithm is
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\ell}_{t}\coloneq\sum_{h}q_{t}^{h}\ell_{t}^{h}=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\ell}_{t}\coloneq\sum_{h}q_{t}^{h}\ell_{t}^{h}=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by linearity.
Using these meta-losses, we define the excess loss of each expert <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}^{h}\coloneq\overline{\ell}_{t}-\ell_{t}^{h}=\left\langle g_{t},x_{t}-x_{t}^{h}\right\rangle/2GD\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}^{h}\coloneq\overline{\ell}_{t}-\ell_{t}^{h}=\left\langle g_{t},x_{t}-x_{t}^{h}\right\rangle/2GD\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These excess losses satisfy <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r_{t}^{h}|\leq\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r_{t}^{h}|\leq\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{h}q_{t}^{h}r_{t}^{h}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{h}q_{t}^{h}r_{t}^{h}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be useful in the regret analysis of
our algorithm.
Finally, the choice of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is aimed at minimizing the meta-regret
against some <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the linear meta-loss <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
since
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left\langle q_{t}-e_{h},\ell_{t}\right\rangle=\sum_{t=1}^{T}(\overline{\ell}_{t}-\ell_{t}^{h})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left\langle q_{t}-e_{h},\ell_{t}\right\rangle=\sum_{t=1}^{T}(\overline{\ell}_{t}-\ell_{t}^{h})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Desired guarantee.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A standard meta-regret bound scaling with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is not sufficient for our purpose, since it would dominate the faster regret guarantees enjoyed by some of the base algorithms. Following second-order expert aggregation methods such as ML-Prod <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaillard and Stoltz, 2014</a>)</cite> and their use in universal OCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, a desirable guarantee compares the meta-algorithm with each expert <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> through its excess losses, and takes the schematic form as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}^{h}\leq{\mathcal{O}}\biggl(\sqrt{\log(1/\rho^{h})\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}}+\log(1/\rho^{h})\biggr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}^{h}\leq{\mathcal{O}}\biggl(\sqrt{\log(1/\rho^{h})\sum\limits_{t=1}^{T}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}}+\log(1/\rho^{h})\biggr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The second-order dependence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial: depending on the loss class, it can either be bounded directly or absorbed by a negative curvature term from the base regret analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With delayed feedback, our goal is to retain this second-order structure while accounting for the additional difficulty caused by outstanding observations. In particular, we seek a delayed analogue in which <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by the locally weighted quantity <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}\chi_{t}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}\chi_{t}(r_{t}^{h})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the maximum number of outstanding observations over the delay interval of round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The following lemma, proven in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Supporting Lemmas ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, provides two complementary
controls on such weighted quadratic terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every real-valued sequence <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{t})_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{t})_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and every sequence of delays,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\chi_{t}v_{t}^{2}\leq\min\left\{(T+2d_{\mathrm{tot}})\max_{t\in[T]}v_{t}^{2},\,\sigma_{\max}^{+}\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_{t}^{2}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\chi_{t}v_{t}^{2}\leq\min\left\{(T+2d_{\mathrm{tot}})\max_{t\in[T]}v_{t}^{2},\,\sigma_{\max}^{+}\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_{t}^{2}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ Desired guarantee. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}=r_{t}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}=r_{t}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, the first bound,
together with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r_{t}^{h}|\leq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r_{t}^{h}|\leq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, controls the weighted quantity by
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T+d_{\mathrm{tot}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T+d_{\mathrm{tot}})</annotation></semantics></math>, providing the total-delay control needed for general
convex losses. The second bound retains the sum of squared excess
losses up to a factor of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving the second-order
structure needed to exploit strong convexity or exp-concavity.
A meta-regret guarantee in terms of this weighted quantity will
therefore allow us to combine the different base algorithms in
<a href="#S4" title="4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, motivating the design below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Algorithm design.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our starting point is ML-Prod <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaillard and Stoltz, 2014</a>)</cite>, whose second-order excess-loss guarantee has exactly the structure motivated above. In the standard non-delayed setting, the excess loss <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is observed immediately after round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and is used to update the same weight vector that generated the prediction. This correspondence is essential to the usual log-potential analysis of ML-Prod. Under delayed feedback, however, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes available only at the end of round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, by which point the weights may already have changed due to feedback from other rounds. Directly applying the ML-Prod update to the current weights therefore breaks this correspondence and, consequently, the usual telescoping argument (see <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E A Brief Review of ML-Prod ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for more details).
A natural alternative is the BOLD reduction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joulani et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, which handles delayed feedback by running independent copies of a non-delayed learner. However, a direct application of BOLD to ML-Prod may require up to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> copies, each paying its own meta-learning cost, and leads to a <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma_{\max}^{+}T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma_{\max}^{+}T}</annotation></semantics></math>-type aggregation bound under the standard analysis, which can be significantly
worse than the refined bounds from <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ Desired guarantee. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> above.
We therefore seek a single meta-algorithm that incorporates all outstanding updates jointly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the weighting principle of Banker-OMD (Huang et al., 2023), we
associate each unresolved round <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in m_{t}\cup\{t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in m_{t}\cup\{t\}</annotation></semantics></math> with a coefficient
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> that specifies how much of its historical weight vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is
kept separately until its feedback arrives. The remaining weight is shared
across rounds and used to construct the current prediction. This decomposition
allows delayed feedback to be applied to the historical weight vector that
generated the corresponding prediction, while still admitting a single
telescoping potential. The resulting procedure is given in <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Algorithm design. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Delayed ML-Prod</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
