<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we leverage the second-order bound from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ Algorithm design. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to
derive near-optimal universal rates for delayed OCO. The
construction proceeds by applying <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Algorithm design. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with a
suitable collection of expert algorithms.
We will construct three
families of base algorithms,
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
corresponding to
strongly-convex, exp-concave, and general convex functions respectively,
each run on an appropriate
sequence of surrogate losses,
and we define the full set of base algorithms to be <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}=\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔄</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}=\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\lceil\log_{2}T\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\lceil\log_{2}T\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda\coloneq\{(2G/D)2^{-j}:j=0,\ldots,J\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\coloneq\{(2G/D)2^{-j}:j=0,\ldots,J\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, define the surrogate loss <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}^{\widehat{\lambda}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle+(\widehat{\lambda}/2)\|x-x_{t}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}^{\widehat{\lambda}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle+(\widehat{\lambda}/2)\|x-x_{t}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{SC}}(\widehat{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{SC}}(\widehat{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> denote an instance of Delayed FTRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> run on the sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}^{\widehat{\lambda}}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}^{\widehat{\lambda}}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}\leq 2G/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}\leq 2G/D</annotation></semantics></math>, these surrogate losses have gradients bounded by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G+\widehat{\lambda}D\leq 3G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G+\widehat{\lambda}D\leq 3G</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define the family of base algorithms for
strongly convex functions as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{SC}}(\widehat{\lambda}):\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{SC}}(\widehat{\lambda}):\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The grid <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees that whenever the original losses are <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex, there exists <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> within a constant factor of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Omitted Details from Section ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For exp-concave losses, let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{B}\coloneq\{(1/8GD)2^{-j}:j=0,\ldots,J\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\coloneq\{(1/8GD)2^{-j}:j=0,\ldots,J\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\beta}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\beta}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the surrogate loss <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}^{\widehat{\beta}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle+(\widehat{\beta}/2)\langle g_{t},x-x_{t}\rangle^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}^{\widehat{\beta}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle+(\widehat{\beta}/2)\langle g_{t},x-x_{t}\rangle^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{EC}}(\widehat{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{EC}}(\widehat{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> denote an instance of <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.1 Improved Regret Bound for Exp-concave Losses ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> run on the sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}^{\widehat{\beta}}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}^{\widehat{\beta}}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, with its internal curvature parameter set to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\beta}\coloneq\widehat{\beta}/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\beta}\coloneq\widehat{\beta}/4</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Omitted Details from Section ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, the surrogate losses have gradients bounded by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(9/8)G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(9/8)G</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfy the quadratic curvature condition required by <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.1 Improved Regret Bound for Exp-concave Losses ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with parameter <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{EC}}(\widehat{\beta}):\widehat{\beta}\in\mathcal{B}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{EC}}(\widehat{\beta}):\widehat{\beta}\in\mathcal{B}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of base algorithms for exp-concave functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, define the convex surrogate <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}^{\mathrm{CVX}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}^{\mathrm{CVX}}(x)\coloneq\langle g_{t},x\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{CVX}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{CVX}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance of delayed OGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi, 2015</a>)</cite> run on the sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}^{\mathrm{CVX}}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}^{\mathrm{CVX}}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since these linear surrogates remain <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, no additional parameter tuning is required, and we set <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{CVX}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}\coloneq\{A_{\mathrm{CVX}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Note that <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{CVX}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{CVX}}</annotation></semantics></math> can obtain <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})</annotation></semantics></math> regret without prior knowledge of <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{tot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{tot}}</annotation></semantics></math> via doubling, as discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi (2015)</a></cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, we define the full collection of base algorithms as <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}\coloneq\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔄</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>SC</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>EC</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>CVX</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}\coloneq\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{SC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{EC}}\cup\mathfrak{A}_{\mathrm{CVX}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the feedback <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> from round <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is revealed, the corresponding surrogate feedback can be reconstructed using the stored master prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the surrogate feedback is available with exactly the same delay as the original gradient feedback.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now the full set of meta-experts used by <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Algorithm design. ‣ 3 Second-order Expert Aggregation with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is then constructed by
associating each base algorithm with a set of meta-learning rates:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=\left\{h=(A,j):A\in\mathfrak{A},j=0,\ldots,J\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=\left\{h=(A,j):A\in\mathfrak{A},j=0,\ldots,J\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=(A,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=(A,j)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an instance of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> associated with
meta-learning rate <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The total number of meta-experts
is then <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathcal{H}\right|=\left|\mathfrak{A}\right|(J+1)=(2J+3)(J+1)=\mathcal{O}(\log^{2}T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>𝔄</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathcal{H}\right|=\left|\mathfrak{A}\right|(J+1)=(2J+3)(J+1)=\mathcal{O}(\log^{2}T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We initialize the meta-algorithm with a uniform prior,
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{h}=1/\left|\mathcal{H}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{h}=1/\left|\mathcal{H}\right|</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote
its entropy by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{\rho}\coloneq\log\left|\mathcal{H}\right|=\log\bigl((J+1)(2J+3)\bigr)=\mathcal{O}(\log\log T)\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{\rho}\coloneq\log\left|\mathcal{H}\right|=\log\bigl((J+1)(2J+3)\bigr)=\mathcal{O}(\log\log T)\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The full algorithm is given in <a href="#algorithm3" title="In 4.2 Universal Regret Guarantee ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>,
and its regret guarantee, proved in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Omitted Details from Section ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>,
is provided by the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Bounded domain and gradients). ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds. Then <a href="#algorithm3" title="In 4.2 Universal Regret Guarantee ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> guarantees the
following:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For arbitrary <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz convex losses,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\big(GD\sqrt{(T+d_{\mathrm{tot}})\log\log T}+GD\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\big(GD\sqrt{(T+d_{\mathrm{tot}})\log\log T}+GD\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex (<a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (𝜆-strong convexity). ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\Big(\tfrac{G^{2}}{\lambda}\left[\log T+\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}+\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\right]\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\Big(\tfrac{G^{2}}{\lambda}\left[\log T+\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}+\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\right]\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-exp-concave (<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (𝛼-exp-concavity). ‣ 2 Problem Setting ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), let
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\min\left\{\frac{1}{4GD},\alpha\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\min\left\{\frac{1}{4GD},\alpha\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math> Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\Big(\tfrac{1}{\beta}\left[n\log T+\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}n\log T,\,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}+\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\right]\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)=\mathcal{O}\Big(\tfrac{1}{\beta}\left[n\log T+\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}n\log T,\,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}+\sigma_{\max}\log\log T\right]\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in the absence of delay, we have <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{tot}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{tot}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The guarantee for the general convex case
then becomes
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(GD\sqrt{T\log\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(GD\sqrt{T\log\log T})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is of the same
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T\log\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T\log\log T})</annotation></semantics></math> order as the worst-case convex guarantee of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Erven (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a></cite>. For strongly convex losses, the bound
reduces to
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}((G^{2}/\lambda)\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}((G^{2}/\lambda)\log T)</annotation></semantics></math>, up to the lower-order
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}((G^{2}/\lambda)\log\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}((G^{2}/\lambda)\log\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> aggregation term, while for
exp-concave losses it reduces to
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}((n/\beta)\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}((n/\beta)\log T)</annotation></semantics></math>, again up to a lower-order
<math id="S4.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\log\log T/\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\log\log T/\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> term. Thus, in the curved regimes, our
no-delay specialization recovers, up to lower-order aggregation terms,
the optimal logarithmic rates achieved by universal methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative approach would be to apply the black-box BOLD reduction of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joulani et al. (2013)</a></cite> to
an undelayed universal base learner. However, recall that
BOLD ensures regret of the form <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)\leq\mathcal{O}(\sigma_{\max}^{+}\operatorname{Reg}^{\mathcal{A}}_{T/\sigma_{\max}^{+}}(u))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Reg</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(u)\leq\mathcal{O}(\sigma_{\max}^{+}\operatorname{Reg}^{\mathcal{A}}_{T/\sigma_{\max}^{+}}(u))</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Reg}^{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Reg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Reg}^{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the regret of the base algorithm.
For convex losses this is
<math id="S4.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\sigma_{\max}^{+}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\sigma_{\max}^{+}T})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be significantly worse than our <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}})</annotation></semantics></math> dependence. For losses with curvature, BOLD may obtain the <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sigma_{\max}^{+}\log(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sigma_{\max}^{+}\log(T))</annotation></semantics></math>
branch of
<math id="S4.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}^{+}\log(T),\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}^{+}\log(T),\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\})</annotation></semantics></math>, but has no mechanism by which to obtain the <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}})</annotation></semantics></math> branch. This difference can be significant when <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is large (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>, Lemma A.9</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The delay dependence also clarifies the relation to existing class-specific delayed algorithms. For general convex losses, our universal guarantee preserves the <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T+d_{\mathrm{tot}}}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence of delayed OGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quanrud and Khashabi, 2015</a>)</cite> up to doubly logarithmic aggregation factors. For strongly convex losses, our universal construction inherits the <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{\sigma_{\max}\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, improving over the <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}\log T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}\log T</annotation></semantics></math> dependence of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2022)</a></cite>. For exp-concave losses, using Delay-Accounting ONS (<a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.1 Improved Regret Bound for Exp-concave Losses ‣ 4 Universal Regret with Delayed Feedback ‣ Universal Online Convex Optimization With Delayed Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as a base learner yields the delay dependence
<math id="S4.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}n\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{\sigma_{\max}n\log T,\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{tot}}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
without knowing the curvature parameter and improving over the result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Thus, across all three regimes, universality preserves the class-specific delay dependencies up to doubly-logarithmic aggregation costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We developed a universal OCO framework for delayed feedback that
adapts automatically to convex, strongly convex, and exp-concave losses
without prior knowledge of the curvature class or parameter. The key
ingredient is a delayed second-order meta-algorithm whose regret can be
controlled through both the total delay and the maximum number of
simultaneously missing observations. Combined with delayed OGD, FTRL, and
ONS base algorithms, this yields class-adaptive regret guarantees up to a
small aggregation overhead. For exp-concave losses, our Delay-Accounting ONS
analysis further replaces the maximum delay <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence in the logarithmic
branch by the potentially smaller quantity <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An interesting direction for future work is to remove the additional
geometric grid over meta-learning rates by developing an adaptive
learning-rate version of the delayed second-order expert aggregation. This could reduce
the number of meta-experts by a factor of <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\log T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It would also be interesting to see whether it is possible to remove knowledge of the timestamps associated with feedback received, while maintaining the strengths
of the guarantees obtained here.</p>
</div>
</section>
